$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Formation of tert-AIkyl Chlorides 5 from tert-Alcohols 1; General
Procedure: Freshly distilled oxalyl chloride (1 ml, 11 mmol) is added at
room temperature to a stirred solution of the alcohol (1 mmol) in dry
benzene (5-10 ml), and the mixture stirred overnight at room temperature.
After evaporation of the voialiles the crude reaction mixture is taken up
in tetrachloromethane (5 ml) and added dropwise to a stirred suspension
of N-hydroxypyridine-2-thione sodium salt (0.17 g, 1.1 mmol) in
tetrachloromethane at reflux. After decolourisation of the thus formed
bright yellow solution, the cooled reaction mixture is filtered on celite
and evaporated to dryness. Filtration through a short silica gel column
gives the pure chlorides (cluant 40-60 petroleum ether) and subsequently
trichloromethyl pyridyl sulphide (6) (eluent dichloromethane), whose
spectra are identical to those of an authentic sample. 5a, yield 85%; 6,
yield 70%. M.p. (°C): 19; Lit. m.p. (°C): 19.68; NMR(CDCl3/TMS) δ (ppm)e:
0.9 (t, 3H), 1.3 (m, 32H), 1.7 (s, 3H).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM A new method for the synthesis of tert-alkyl chlorides from tert-alcohols
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Crich, David; Fortt, Simon M.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synthesis (1987), (1), 35-7

